# Rule 110, the elementary cellular automaton, with its space-time diagram
# left behind on the tape. Rule 110 is Turing complete, so this is a
# completeness argument by citation, where tm.rw makes one by simulation.
#
#   rw progs/rule110.rw '!00000001>####' | tr / '\n'
#
# Input is "!" then the starting row in 0s and 1s, then ">", then one "#" per
# generation to run. Output is every generation separated by "/".
#
# The awkward part on a one-dimensional tape is that the old row and the new
# row want to live in the same span. Overwriting in place destroys the history
# the diagram is made of, and interleaving the two rows only postpones the
# problem -- separating them afterwards costs a quadratic shuffle.
#
# So the new cells are not written where they are computed. The sweep marker
# (P = cell to my left was 0, Q = it was 1) walks rightwards over the old row,
# leaving it untouched, and each cell it computes is launched as a traveller
# (t = a new 0, u = a new 1) that runs to the far end of the tape and lands
# just before the "." terminator. The old row stays put as history, and the
# new row assembles itself in order beyond the ">".
#
# Only one traveller is ever in flight: the traveller rules sit above the
# sweep rules, so while a traveller is moving it always matches first and the
# sweep cannot take another step. That single fact is what keeps the new
# cells in order -- without it two travellers could overtake each other and
# the row would come out shuffled.

# bootstrap -- fires once each, then never matches again
!-><[
>#->>.

# travellers in flight. First, so that a moving traveller freezes the sweep.
t0->0t
t1->1t
t>->>t
t.->0.
u0->0u
u1->1u
u>->>u
u.->1.

# the sweep: "P c r" -> keep c, advance the marker, launch f(prev,c,r).
# 110 is "1 unless the neighbourhood is 111, 100 or 000".
P00->0Pt0
P01->0Pu1
P10->1Qu0
P11->1Qu1
Q00->0Pt0
Q01->0Pu1
Q10->1Qu0
Q11->1Qt1

# last cell of the row: the right neighbour is the boundary, which is 0
P0>->0Et>
P1>->1Eu>
Q0>->0Et>
Q1>->1Eu>

# row finished. The old ">" becomes the new row's left bracket, and K walks
# out to the counter to spend a generation.
E>->[K
K0->0K
K1->1K
K.#->>.
K.->V

# counter empty: V walks home, tidying the scaffolding out of the output
0V->V0
1V->V1
/V->V/
[V->/V
<V/->.
<V->.

# next generation. Below the V rules, or cleanup would start a fresh sweep.
[0->/P0
[1->/P1
